  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-heisenberg-lie-algebra": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $k$ be a field and $n\\geq 1$. The **Heisenberg Lie algebra** $\\mathfrak h_n(k)$ is the $(2n+1)$-dimensional $k$-vector space with basis\n\n$$X_1,\\ldots,X_n,P_1,\\ldots,P_n,Z.$$\n\nIts bracket is the bilinear extension of\n\n$$[P_i,X_j]=\\delta_{ij}Z,\\qquad [X_i,P_j]=-\\delta_{ij}Z,$$\n\nwith all $[X_i,X_j]$, $[P_i,P_j]$, $[Z,X_i]$, and $[Z,P_i]$ equal to zero. Here $\\delta_{ij}=1$ when $i=j$ and $0$ otherwise. Equivalently, for coefficient vectors $a,b,a',b'\\in k^n$,\n\n$$\\left[\\sum_i(a_iX_i+b_iP_i)+cZ,\\sum_i(a_i'X_i+b_i'P_i)+c'Z\\right]=\\left(\\sum_i(b_i a_i'-b_i'a_i)\\right)Z.$$\n\nThe Jacobi identity and alternation, including in characteristic $2$, are proved in [[lem-heisenberg-bracket-defines-lie-algebra]]."
    ],
    "def-polynomial-weyl-algebra": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $k$ be a field, $n\\geq 1$, and $\\hbar\\in k$. Let $E_n$ be the $2n$-dimensional $k$-vector space with basis $x_1,\\ldots,x_n,p_1,\\ldots,p_n$, and form its tensor algebra $T(E_n)$ ([[def-tensor-algebra-of-a-vector-space]]). Let $J_{n,\\hbar}$ be the two-sided ideal generated by\n\n$$x_ix_j-x_jx_i,\\qquad p_ip_j-p_jp_i,\\qquad p_ix_j-x_jp_i-\\hbar\\delta_{ij}1\\quad(1\\leq i,j\\leq n).$$\n\nThe **polynomial Weyl algebra with central parameter $\\hbar$** is\n\n$$W_n(k;\\hbar):=T(E_n)/J_{n,\\hbar},$$\n\nwhere the quotient is the ring quotient from [[def-quotient-ring]] and $J_{n,\\hbar}$ is the generated ideal from [[def-generated-and-principal-ideals]]. The standard $n$th polynomial Weyl algebra is $A_n(k):=W_n(k;1)$. The phrase “polynomial Weyl algebra” here means this algebraic associative quotient; no topology, completion, or unitary exponential generators are part of the definition.\n\nThe exact presentation, normal-ordered basis, and equivalence with the central quotient of $U(\\mathfrak h_n(k))$ are established in [[thm-polynomial-weyl-algebra-normal-ordered-basis]]."
    ],
    "lem-trace-of-commutators-of-square-matrices-is-zero": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $R$ be a commutative ring, let $d\\geq 0$, and let $A,B\\in M_d(R)$. Then\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(AB-BA)=0.$$"
    ],
    "lem-heisenberg-bracket-defines-lie-algebra": [
      "## Statement\n\nFor every field $k$ and integer $n\\geq 1$, the vector space and bracket specified in [[def-heisenberg-lie-algebra]] form a Lie algebra over $k$."
    ],
    "thm-no-nonzero-finite-dimensional-weyl-modules-in-characteristic-zero": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $k$ be a field of characteristic zero, let $n\\geq 1$, and let $0\\ne\\hbar\\in k$. Then the polynomial Weyl algebra $W_n(k;\\hbar)$ has no nonzero finite-dimensional unital left module over $k$."
    ],
    "thm-polynomial-weyl-algebra-normal-ordered-basis": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $k$ be any field, $n\\geq 1$, and $\\hbar\\in k$. Let $\\mathfrak h_n(k)$ and $W_n(k;\\hbar)$ be as in [[def-heisenberg-lie-algebra]] and [[def-polynomial-weyl-algebra]]. Since $Z$ is central, define the quotient $Q_\\hbar:=U(\\mathfrak h_n(k))/(Z-\\hbar1)$.\n\nThen the assignment\n\n$$X_i\\longmapsto x_i,\\qquad P_i\\longmapsto p_i,\\qquad Z\\longmapsto \\hbar 1$$\n\nidentifies the polynomial Weyl algebra with the central quotient\n\n$$W_n(k;\\hbar)\\cong U(\\mathfrak h_n(k))/(Z-\\hbar 1).$$\n\nThe normal-ordered elements\n\n$$x^\\alpha p^\\beta:=x_1^{\\alpha_1}\\cdots x_n^{\\alpha_n}p_1^{\\beta_1}\\cdots p_n^{\\beta_n}\\qquad(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb N^n)$$\n\nform a $k$-basis of $W_n(k;\\hbar)$ for every characteristic. Equivalently, in the presentation of [[def-polynomial-weyl-algebra]], the relations rewrite to these unique normal forms. If $\\hbar\\ne0$, scaling $p_i\\mapsto\\hbar p_i$ gives $W_n(k;\\hbar)\\cong A_n(k)$; if $\\hbar=0$, the defining generators commute."
    ],
    "lem-common-domain-commutator-integrates-to-weyl-relations": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $A_1,\\ldots,A_m$ be self-adjoint operators on a complex Hilbert space $H$, where $1\\le m<\\infty$, and let $c=(c_{ab})$ be a real skew-symmetric matrix. Define $U_a(s)=e^{isA_a}$ by the Borel functional calculus. Suppose a complex linear subspace $D\\subset H$ has the following properties:\n\n- $D$ is a core for every $A_b$: it is contained in $D(A_b)$ and is dense there for the graph norm $\\|\\xi\\|+\\|A_b\\xi\\|$. In particular it is dense in $H$.\n- $A_aD\\subset D$ and $U_a(s)D=D$ for every $a$ and every real $s$.\n- On $D$, $(A_aA_b-A_bA_a)\\xi=i c_{ab}\\xi$ for all $a,b$.\n- For every $a,b$ and $\\xi\\in D$, the orbit $s\\mapsto U_a(s)\\xi$ is differentiable in the graph norm of $A_b$, with derivative $iA_aU_a(s)\\xi$. Explicitly, in addition to the Hilbert-space derivative, the required limit is\n\n$$\\lim_{h\\to0}\\left\\|\\frac{A_bU_a(s+h)\\xi-A_bU_a(s)\\xi}{h}-iA_bA_aU_a(s)\\xi\\right\\|=0.$$\n\nThese are sufficient regularity hypotheses, stronger than a commutator identity on a common invariant dense domain alone. Then, as bounded operators on all of $H$,\n\n$$U_a(s)U_b(t)=e^{-ic_{ab}st}U_b(t)U_a(s)\\qquad(s,t\\in\\mathbb R).$$\n\nFor $f\\in\\mathbb R^m$ put\n\n$$W(f)=\\exp\\left(\\frac i2\\sum_{a<b}c_{ab}f_af_b\\right)U_1(f_1)\\cdots U_m(f_m),\\qquad \\sigma(f,g)=\\sum_{a,b}f_ac_{ab}g_b.$$\n\nThen $W$ is strongly continuous, unitary-valued, $W(0)=I$, and\n\n$$W(f)W(g)=e^{-i\\sigma(f,g)/2}W(f+g),\\qquad W(f)^*=W(-f).$$\n\nIn particular, taking $m=2n$, $A=(Q_1,\\ldots,Q_n,P_1,\\ldots,P_n)$ and $c=\\kappa\\begin{pmatrix}0&I_n\\\\-I_n&0\\end{pmatrix}$ gives the finite-mode exponentiated Weyl relations at any real central parameter $\\kappa$. If $\\kappa\\ne0$, the restrictions $x_j=Q_j|_D$ and $p_j=(i/\\kappa)P_j|_D$ give an algebraic $A_n(\\mathbb C)$-module on $D$ in the normalization of [[def-polynomial-weyl-algebra]]. The conclusion applies to these eligible Hilbert-space realizations; it asserts no exponentiation for arbitrary algebraic modules, universal $C^*$-completion, or uniqueness of representations."
    ],
    "rem-algebraic-weyl-modules-do-not-automatically-exponentiate": [],
    "ex-truncated-polynomial-weyl-module-in-characteristic-p": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $k$ be a field of characteristic $p>0$, where $p$ is prime. On $M=k[t]/(t^p)$, multiplication by $t$ and formal differentiation induce a nonzero $p$-dimensional unital left module over $A_1(k)=W_1(k;1)$."
    ],
    "ex-polynomial-differential-representation-of-weyl-algebra": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $k$ be any field and $n\\geq1$. Multiplication by $t_i$ and partial differentiation $\\partial_i$ define a unital left $A_n(k)$-module structure on $k[t_1,\\ldots,t_n]$. If $\\operatorname{char}(k)=p>0$, this representation is not faithful: $p_i^p$ is a nonzero element of $A_n(k)$ that acts by zero."
    ]
  }
}
